(C) The named suspicion — is `CENSUS_COMPLETE` reachable?

REACHABLE. Routing:

1. §3 routes a claim into a non-arithmetic class only when a terminal condition holds: no derivation stated, a `BLOCKED` input, an `ABSENT` input, or a stalled/unsupported evaluation. A claim whose derivation is stated and whose inputs are all `PRINTED`/`STANDARD` has no route except the arithmetic group — the precedence list itself ends "then the arithmetic group."
2. §4.2 files `CENSUS_PARTIAL` iff "at least one included claim carries a non-arithmetic outcome"; §4.1 files `CENSUS_COMPLETE` iff every included claim carries an arithmetic-group outcome. Which files is a function of the corpus, not of any textual bar.
3. A single blocked, absent-input, or no-derivation claim anywhere in the corpus does force `CENSUS_PARTIAL` (and PARTIAL precedes COMPLETE in the §4 order). So COMPLETE is fragile — in a real corpus of many papers it may never be filed. But reachability asks whether an input exists, and one does: a corpus in which every included claim states its derivation and states or standards every input. On that input, §3 files only arithmetic-group outcomes and every earlier §4 limb is false, so the final limb files `CENSUS_COMPLETE`.
4. No clause blocks every path. The document's own C0 note anticipates exactly this: COMPLETE "a single blocked or absent input in the whole corpus is enough to prevent" — enough to prevent on that corpus, not on every corpus. Both V9 C0 seats independently found `CENSUS_COMPLETE` reachable (§10.3).

